//! # Language basics
//!
//! A Hew program starts in `fn main()`. Bind immutable values with `let` and
//! mutable values with `var`; Hew has no `let mut` form. Primitive types use
//! lowercase names: signed and unsigned fixed-width integers, `usize`, `f32`,
//! `f64`, `bool`, `char`, `string`, and `bytes`. Tuples, fixed arrays,
//! `Vec<T>`, maps, and sets compose those values with user-defined records and
//! enums.
//!
//! ```hew
//! type Reading {
//!     label: string;
//!     value: i64;
//! }
//!
//! enum Level {
//!     Normal;
//!     High(i64);
//! }
//!
//! fn classify(reading: Reading) -> Level {
//!     if reading.value > 80 {
//!         .High(reading.value)
//!     } else {
//!         .Normal
//!     }
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     var readings: Vec<Reading> = Vec.new();
//!     readings.push(Reading { label: "cpu", value: 92 });
//!
//!     for reading in readings {
//!         let label = reading.label;
//!         match classify(reading) {
//!             .Normal => println(f"{label}: normal"),
//!             .High(value) => println(f"{label}: high ({value})"),
//!         }
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `type` declares a record; there is no `struct` keyword. Enum variants end
//! with semicolons and can be constructed with a leading dot when the expected
//! enum type is known. `match` is exhaustive, so adding a variant requires its
//! callers to handle the new case.
//!
//! A block's last expression is its value. A trailing semicolon makes that
//! expression a statement returning unit. Use `if` or `match` wherever an
//! expression is accepted.
//!
//! ## Functions, ownership, and errors
//!
//! Parameters are borrowed or copied according to their type. Write
//! `consume value: T` when a public function takes ownership, and `var self`
//! for a method that mutates its receiver. Functions declared with
//! `fails ErrorType` are handled with `match`, `?`, or `handle error { ... }`.
//! `Option<T>` represents an optional value and `Result<T, E>` represents an
//! explicit success or error value; their methods are listed in the standard
//! library reference.
//!
//! ## Modules and imports
//!
//! Import modules with dotted paths, for example `import std.encoding.json;`.
//! The module can then be used through its final path component, such as
//! `json.parse(text)`. Use `hew check file.hew` to type-check a standalone
//! program before building or running it.
//!
